
-----------------------------------------------------
TreeMap
-----------------------------------------------------
1. TreeMap stores data in sorted order
Automatically sorts keys in ascending order (natural order)
Example: 1, 2, 3 or A, B, C
🔥 2. Sorting is based on keys (not values)
TreeMap always sorts by key only
Values have no role in ordering
🔥 3. Uses Red-Black Tree internally
TreeMap is implemented using a Self-balancing Red-Black Tree
So operations are always balanced
🔥 4. Time complexity is O(log n)
put(), get(), remove() → O(log n)
Slower than HashMap (O(1)) but sorted
🔥 5. Null key is NOT allowed
❌ TreeMap does not allow null keys
Because it needs to compare keys for sorting
🔥 6. Null values ARE allowed
✔ You can store multiple null values
Only keys restriction exists
🔥 7. Custom sorting using Comparator
You can define your own sorting logic
new TreeMap<>(Comparator.reverseOrder());

🔥 8. Implements NavigableMap interface
Supports navigation methods like:
ceilingKey()
floorKey()
higherKey()
lowerKey()
🔥 9. Not synchronized (not thread-safe)
TreeMap is not thread-safe
Use Collections.synchronizedMap() if needed
🔥 10. Best when sorted order is required

Use TreeMap when:

You need sorted keys
You need range operations
You need navigation features

-----------------------------------------
1. NavigableMap is a sub-interface of SortedMap in Java
2. It provides navigation methods to move through keys in a sorted map
3. It is implemented by TreeMap
4. Keys are always stored in sorted order (ascending by default)
5. It provides methods to get closest matches for a given key
6. ceilingKey() returns smallest key ≥ given key
7. floorKey() returns largest key ≤ given key
8. higherKey() returns smallest key > given key
9. lowerKey() returns largest key < given key
10. It is useful when we need range-based searching and navigation in maps
